Think slow: 

our minds can spin, spin, spin so fast, emotion pressing that pedal down before your mind gets a foot in the door. It is human nature to form hypotheses and make assumptions. It is how our ancestors survived in an environment that often provided incomplete information, where intuiting ground truth through pattern recognition was a matter of life and death.

Sometimes it still matters today, but the human mind, as sophisticated and beautiful as it is, can get ahead of itself, misunderstand itself, and tie itself into knots of conflicting signals, especially in an environment it never evolved to inhabit. Our new habitat—civilization—is constantly changing beneath our feet, and without the proper tools it is easy for us to lose our footing.

This is why we need to slow down: in mind, in body, in breath. We need to slow down. We need to make it a skill. We need to stalk our ideas and sneak up on them like NINJAS!

So much of life and the social environment presses this neurotic demand for speed, when in fact speed generates errors that inevitably require you to backtrack and unfuck all your mistakes, sometimes tripling the amount of time it takes. If you had just gone half as fast, you would have outperformed.

The more you can avoid clinging to your thoughts, the more you can slow things down, meet that feeling of urgency or eureka, passively observe it, and let go of the compulsion. The more of a chance that thought has to ripen.

It is not going anywhere. It is encoded in narrative, and simply revisiting the narrative will give that thought new life and perspective. They say human memories can change when we recall them. Inversely, what is perceived as a defect can become a strength: every time you recall something, you have the opportunity to gain new insight.

You write down a series of reflections on the same insight, each from a different frame of reference, and then you can begin to discern the shape of truth from its shadow.

Slow the fuck down.”

